@misc{zeddelta2026,
      title={Introducing Delta},
      author={{Zed Industries}},
      year={2026},
      url={https://zed.dev/blog/introducing-delta},
      note={Blog survey 2026-08-13 (post of 2026-08-12 — the ink is wet; scored on the
            announcement, not on docs). Fourth co-genesis point, from the editor side, and
            the closest one yet on diagnosis. Two of their three named pain points are ours:
            the durable unit is the state *between* commits (git discards it, collaboration
            happens in it — our §3.6 "work-in-progress is real state, not a dirty buffer"),
            and review context goes stale the moment code moves beneath it (our §4.4 stale
            approvals). DeltaDB replicates the worktree AND the conversation together, live,
            per participant; git stays canonical underneath; comments anchor to code and
            survive edits; history stays with the code. CRDTs unnamed in the body but the
            post roots itself in zed.dev/blog/crdts.
            THE CONTRAST WORTH KEEPING: same diagnosis, opposite repair. They *anchor* — the
            comment tracks moving code, preserving location and staying legible. We *pin* —
            the approval records the head it endorsed, preserving the claim and exposing the
            drift. An anchored comment on drifted code still reads as endorsement of work
            its author never saw. Not substitutes; the composition is anchor-for-legibility
            plus pin-for-accountability, and only the second is a substrate guarantee.
            Second contrast: replicating the draft is a third position beyond destruction
            and multiverse (§5.4.1) — divergence is prevented rather than resolved, which
            works precisely when no actor reasons long enough to need a snapshot protected.
            Stops short where the others do: no basis (○ by construction — a live-replicated
            draft has no prior state to name), no reader currency, no audience topology, no
            adjudication; collaboration is humans + one agent, so multi-actor contention does
            not yet arise. Placed: §7 (co-genesis), §4.4 (anchoring vs pinning), §5.4.1
            (third position), Table 1 "Replicated worktree" row + note d.},
}
